{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6sqXxW4yezGmcjpyDjvDz","type":"tool_result","content":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex c77352ce..0635f3d5 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -369,7 +369,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `lint-spec` — verifies each `spec.md` section has real content, not placeholders. **`--ready-check`** applies the **Definition of Ready** (ENTRY gate): Intent/Requirements/Technical Plan filled, acceptance criteria with stable IDs (`- R<N>:`) and syntactically valid `depends_on` — without requiring Validation/Final Report. The Definition of Ready is opt-in: it applies to specs created on or after `adopted_at` in `.pose/policy/dor.json` (shipped empty), and `task_type` in the spec's frontmatter selects which sections it requires; once adopted, `check` applies it on the `→ in-progress` transition. Use `--all` to audit every spec; `--required-only` skips the optional `Decisions` section. **Lifecycle gate:** `status: done` requires `completed_at` and a valid disposition on every follow-up; for `spawned`/`covered`/`duplicate` the target must be an **existing** spec (and not itself). A target in another project is written qualified, `[covered: xref:<project>/spec:<slug>]`: when that project is bound (`POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS`) the spec must exist there, and when it is not bound the target is accepted, because the other repository being absent is not evidence the spec is missing. In any status it warns when an open follow-up's ownership is written outside the trailing `(owner:@alias crit:low|medium|high review:YYYY-MM-DD)` group, and it reads a wrapped follow-up bullet as one item, like `followups`.\n - `followups` — aggregates all specs' follow-ups, derives the live (`--open`) or full (`--all`) backlog, projects ownership (`--owner <alias>`) and expired reviews (`--overdue`), and proposes near-duplicate candidates by deterministic lexical similarity (token Jaccard + `SequenceMatcher`, stdlib only; threshold via `--similarity 0..100`, default 60). Exit 0 by default, no network; `--fail-overdue` turns expired reviews into a blocking, risk-based policy gate. The candidates are mechanical hints — the **semantic judgement** and the **confirmation of reuse** live in the agent layer (skill `pose-spec-closeout`), never in this script. Source coverage (spec `pose-source-coverage-integrity`): the read reports what it could not see. `--json` carries `coverage_complete` and `coverage` (each loss names the artifact, the kind, and the spec it belongs to when the file name or a readable header shows it), the human output prints `coverage=INCOMPLETE` and each unread file, and `--fail-overdue` fails instead of certifying \"none overdue\" over an incomplete read. The backlog, `--candidates`, the project state and `pose state --attention` count one unresolved set: an explicit `[open]` and a bullet with no disposition (Attention projects the latter as `followup-undisposed`). The same applies to `pose assess tech-debt`, whose report states the files scanned, the ones it could not read (incomplete) and the ones it leaves out on purpose (over the size limit, binary, generated), and finds a marker after a very long line; and to the review history: `pose review-check` and `pose review verify` do not take the latest readable attempt or attestation as the current one while an unreadable record that may be later exists, and `pose review record` refuses to choose the attempt it supersedes from a history with a hole. The obligation report marks a producer `incomplete` when it lost an element (an obligation refused by validation, a spec or review record it could not read).\n - `review-plan` / `review-check` / `closeout-check` — resolve a deterministic component-aware plan from governed metadata, typed overlays and a closed native-tool catalog, then validate immutable attempts against both scope and plan digests. Opt in explicitly with review-policy schema v2, `component_aware: true` and `component_aware_adopted_at`; preview the migration read-only with `pose review-plan <scope> --explain` before committing the policy. Completed attempts older than that component adoption date remain auditable, while open scopes require the new plan. `pose review record` is dry-run by default, accepts `--plan-digest` to reject drift and appends with `--apply`; recommended tools are never executed implicitly. Hierarchical closeout rolls approvals through `spec:`, `milestone:` and `roadmap:` scopes. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_plan`, `pose_closeout_state`. The JSON of `closeout-check` and `pose_closeout_state` types its blockers in `diagnostics` (code, domain, refs, satisfaction condition; text blockers the engine cannot type yet are carried as `opaque`) and its next action in `next_step`; readiness `waiting_on` entries carry a `code`, and auto-attest pendencies a `code`, `source` and `condition` (spec `pose-typed-producer-diagnostics`). The prose fields stay for people; consumers read the codes.\n-- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` needs a severity and an action, and `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n+- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by|limit]` needs a severity and an action, `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date, and `within-limit` needs the id of a sealed accepted limit as `limit` and a rationale. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n   **Raw history:** every history reader of structural review and of the delivery graph ignores replace refs and grafts; a repository with either gets other review inputs than before, so rerun `pose index` and reseal. A trailer change set that starts at the repository's first commit resolves against the empty tree.\n   **Removal witnesses:** a review subject path a later spec removed reads as that removal only while Git still shows it: the deleting commit on `HEAD`, read raw, with no later commit touching the path, in a clone that is not shallow. Otherwise preparation refuses and asks for `pose index`. A generation binds the change sets of `.pose/reports/history` as `recorded-change-sets`, so one published earlier reads as unbound until the next `pose index`.\n   **Children:** a milestone of one local member has no review of its own and stands on that spec's closed approval, and a roadmap may declare `unreviewed_milestones` for milestones whose members are all terminal, sealed by its own review. Otherwise a milestone or roadmap's approval stands only while the review of each child does: a child rejected, stale, with lost attestation history, or not closed under a done parent stops the parent verifying approved, in `review verify`, `review-check` and closeout, and the parent recovers with its child, with no reseal. Selection follows replacement lineage: a replacement of the same content comes before what it replaces whatever the seal times say, a `replaces` naming other content counts for nothing, and a replaced bundle's approval is not retained.\ndiff --git a/docs-site/docs/cli.md b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\nindex 212c45f8..287e9081 100644\n--- a/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n+++ b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n@@ -136,13 +136,13 @@ as current, but named so a reviewer can tell carried-forward evidence apart.\n - `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records a disposition for a\n   required criterion: `passed`, `not-applicable` (a rationale is required) or\n   `finding` (the evidence slot names a finding this attestation records).\n-- `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]`\n+- `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by|limit]`\n   records a finding. Every finding needs a severity and an action. `resolved`\n   and `wont-fix` close it; `accepted-risk` closes it only for a severity the\n   sealed gates accept and only with an owner, a rationale and a review date;\n   `within-limit` closes it at any severity, only with the id of a limit the\n-  spec declares under `### Accepted limits` and the bundle sealed (its\n-  `limit` field) and a rationale; `open` and `changes-requested` block\n+  spec declares under `### Accepted limits` and the bundle sealed (the ninth\n+  field, `limit`) and a rationale; `open` and `changes-requested` block\n   closeout. A reviewer records it from a follow-up whose `limit` names that\n   sealed limit.\n - The decision must be `approved`, or `approved-with-reservations` where the\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\nindex 973059fc..81db72b0 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\n@@ -101,6 +101,7 @@ func cmdReviewCheck(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {\n \tif eval.Assurance != nil {\n \t\trender(stdout, stderr).Field(\"review.assurance\", posemodel.RenderReviewAssurance(*eval.Assurance))\n \t}\n+\trenderWithinLimitFindings(render(stdout, stderr), \"review\", eval.Current)\n \tfor _, warning := range eval.Warnings {\n \t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"[WARN] %s\\n\", warning)\n \t}\n@@ -324,6 +325,7 @@ func cmdCloseoutCheck(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int\n \tif state.Review.Assurance != nil {\n \t\trender(stdout, stderr).Field(\"closeout.review_assurance\", posemodel.RenderReviewAssurance(*state.Review.Assurance))\n \t}\n+\trenderWithinLimitFindings(render(stdout, stderr), \"closeout\", state.Review.Current)\n \tfor _, blocker := range state.Blockers {\n \t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, \"[ERROR] %s\\n\", blocker)\n \t}\n@@ -1274,7 +1276,7 @@ func parseCLIReviewFindings(values []string) ([]posemodel.ReviewFinding, error)\n \tfor _, raw := range values {\n \t\tparts := strings.Split(raw, \"|\")\n \t\tif len(parts) < 5 || posemodel.ValidateSlug(parts[0]) != nil {\n-\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"finding must be ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]\")\n+\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"finding must be ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by|limit]\")\n \t\t}\n \t\tfinding := posemodel.ReviewFinding{ID: parts[0], Severity: parts[1], Disposition: parts[2], Action: parts[3], Evidence: parts[4]}\n \t\tif len(parts) > 5 {\n@@ -1286,6 +1288,11 @@ func parseCLIReviewFindings(values []string) ([]posemodel.ReviewFinding, error)\n \t\tif len(parts) > 7 {\n \t\t\tfinding.ReviewBy = parts[7]\n \t\t}\n+\t\t// The accepted limit a within-limit finding falls inside (spec\n+\t\t// pose-review-declared-limits-followups R3).\n+\t\tif len(parts) > 8 {\n+\t\t\tfinding.Limit = parts[8]\n+\t\t}\n \t\tresult = append(result, finding)\n \t}\n \treturn result, nil\n@@ -2596,3 +2603,17 @@ func closeGateBlocker(root string, scope posemodel.ScopeRef) string {\n \t}\n \treturn \"\"\n }\n+\n+// renderWithinLimitFindings shows, in the text output, each finding the\n+// current review recorded within a declared accepted limit, as the JSON\n+// does (spec pose-review-declared-limits-followups R6).\n+func renderWithinLimitFindings(out *cliout.Renderer, prefix string, current *posemodel.ReviewAttempt) {\n+\tif current == nil {\n+\t\treturn\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, f := range current.Findings {\n+\t\tif f.Disposition == \"within-limit\" {\n+\t\t\tout.Field(prefix+\".within_limit\", fmt.Sprintf(\"%s limit:%s severity:%s rationale:%s\", f.ID, f.Limit, f.Severity, strings.ReplaceAll(f.Rationale, \"\\n\", \" \")))\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\nindex 713f2261..5702a69a 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n@@ -79,11 +79,19 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].bad = true\n \t\t}\n \t}\n-\ttext := body\n-\tif start := strings.Index(text, \"<!--\"); start >= 0 && !strings.Contains(text[start:], \"-->\") {\n+\t// Any unterminated comment, not only the first, hides what follows it\n+\t// (spec pose-review-declared-limits-followups R2).\n+\tif unterminatedHTMLComment(body) {\n \t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: an unterminated HTML comment hides the rest of the spec\")\n \t}\n-\ttext = stripHTMLComments(text)\n+\t// A comment inside the section, also one written in inline code, would\n+\t// change a limit's sealed text: the section is refused whole instead of\n+\t// guessing which is meant (R2, D1).\n+\tif commentInAcceptedLimitsSection(body) {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: the section holds an HTML comment marker; write the limits without comments\")\n+\t\treturn nil, problems\n+\t}\n+\ttext := stripLimitComments(body)\n \tfence := \"\"\n \tfor _, line := range strings.Split(text, \"\\n\") {\n \t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n@@ -103,6 +111,12 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \t\tif fence != \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif in && indent > 0 && len(entries) > 0 && indent > entries[len(entries)-1].indent && indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\t// A heading-like line indented under a limit: read as a heading it\n+\t\t\t// would end the section and cut the limit (R1).\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is a heading inside a limit; write the limit as one item\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n \t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(trimmed, \"# \"))\n \t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n@@ -215,3 +229,94 @@ func sealedAcceptedLimit(bundle ReviewBundle, id string) bool {\n \t}\n \treturn false\n }\n+\n+// htmlCommentStart returns the index in s, from from on, of the next \"<!--\"\n+// that opens an HTML comment, or -1. One written in inline code (an odd\n+// number of backticks before it on its line) opens nothing: Markdown shows\n+// it as text, as a spec that names the marker does (review of\n+// pose-review-declared-limits-followups: its own R2 named it).\n+func htmlCommentStart(s string, from int) int {\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tk := strings.Index(s[from:], \"<!--\")\n+\t\tif k < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn -1\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tk += from\n+\t\tlineStart := strings.LastIndex(s[:k], \"\\n\") + 1\n+\t\tif strings.Count(s[lineStart:k], \"`\")%2 == 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn k\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfrom = k + 4\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// stripLimitComments removes the HTML comments of body, as Markdown hides\n+// them; an unterminated one hides the rest.\n+func stripLimitComments(body string) string {\n+\tvar b strings.Builder\n+\tfrom := 0\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tstart := htmlCommentStart(body, from)\n+\t\tif start < 0 {\n+\t\t\tb.WriteString(body[from:])\n+\t\t\treturn b.String()\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tb.WriteString(body[from:start])\n+\t\tend := strings.Index(body[start+4:], \"-->\")\n+\t\tif end < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn b.String()\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfrom = start + 4 + end + 3\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// unterminatedHTMLComment reports whether any comment body opens has no\n+// \"-->\" after it.\n+func unterminatedHTMLComment(body string) bool {\n+\tfrom := 0\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tstart := htmlCommentStart(body, from)\n+\t\tif start < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn false\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tend := strings.Index(body[start+4:], \"-->\")\n+\t\tif end < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn true\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfrom = start + 4 + end + 3\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// commentInAcceptedLimitsSection reports whether a line of the accepted\n+// limits section, read before comments are stripped, holds a comment marker.\n+// A comment outside the section, and a section heading inside one, are not\n+// the section.\n+func commentInAcceptedLimitsSection(body string) bool {\n+\tin, inComment := false, false\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(body, \"\\n\") {\n+\t\tif inComment {\n+\t\t\tk := strings.Index(line, \"-->\")\n+\t\t\tif k < 0 {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tinComment = false\n+\t\t\tline = line[k+3:]\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif in && (strings.Contains(line, \"<!--\") || strings.Contains(line, \"-->\")) {\n+\t\t\treturn true\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif k := htmlCommentStart(line, 0); k >= 0 {\n+\t\t\tif !strings.Contains(line[k:], \"-->\") {\n+\t\t\t\tinComment = true\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n+\t\tindent := len(line) - len(strings.TrimLeft(line, \" \\t\"))\n+\t\tif indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(trimmed, \"# \"))\n+\t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn false\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\nindex 056373dc..f835ba24 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n@@ -181,7 +181,11 @@ func reviewAttestationFromDraft(run ReviewRun, bundle ReviewBundle, draft Review\n \t\t// A follow-up the reviewer classified inside a declared accepted\n \t\t// limit is recorded as such, naming the limit (spec\n \t\t// pose-review-declared-threat-model R3).\n-\t\tif f.Disposition == \"follow-up\" && f.Limit != \"\" {\n+\t\t// Only a limit the bundle sealed: a draft of any decision naming\n+\t\t// another is recorded as the follow-up it otherwise is, so no\n+\t\t// attestation names a limit that does not exist (spec\n+\t\t// pose-review-declared-limits-followups R4).\n+\t\tif f.Disposition == \"follow-up\" && f.Limit != \"\" && sealedAcceptedLimit(bundle, f.Limit) {\n \t\t\tfinding.Disposition, finding.Limit, finding.Rationale, finding.Owner, finding.ReviewBy = \"within-limit\", f.Limit, f.Summary, f.Owner, f.ReviewBy\n \t\t}\n \t\tatt.Findings = append(att.Findings, finding)\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json\nindex f5d485d4..d30f8088 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json\n+++ b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json\n@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@\n         \"plan\": {\n           \"type\": \"object\",\n           \"required\": [\"plan_digest\", \"independence\", \"components\", \"criteria\", \"tools\"],\n-          \"properties\": {\"plan_digest\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/digest\"}}\n+          \"properties\": {\"plan_digest\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/digest\"}, \"limitations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"accepted_limits\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"$ref\": \"review-plan.schema.json#/$defs/acceptedLimit\"}}}\n         },\n         \"gates\": {\n           \"type\": \"object\",\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\nindex dc28d2d5..32653ca1 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\n+++ b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\n@@ -25,9 +25,17 @@\n     \"structure\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/structure\"},\n     \"warnings\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n     \"blockers\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n-    \"explain\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}\n+    \"explain\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n+    \"limitations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n+    \"accepted_limits\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/acceptedLimit\"}}\n   },\n   \"$defs\": {\n+    \"acceptedLimit\": {\n+      \"type\": \"object\",\n+      \"additionalProperties\": true,\n+      \"required\": [\"id\", \"text\"],\n+      \"properties\": {\"id\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^([a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*#)?L[1-9][0-9]*$\"}, \"text\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"spec\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}\n+    },\n     \"component\": {\n       \"type\": \"object\",\n       \"additionalProperties\": true,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"a594d0f6-9f6c-4345-9da1-b0d391a3c73f","timestamp":"2026-11-27T12:02:15.928Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex c77352ce..0635f3d5 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -369,7 +369,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n - `lint-spec` — verifies each `spec.md` section has real content, not placeholders. **`--ready-check`** applies the **Definition of Ready** (ENTRY gate): Intent/Requirements/Technical Plan filled, acceptance criteria with stable IDs (`- R<N>:`) and syntactically valid `depends_on` — without requiring Validation/Final Report. The Definition of Ready is opt-in: it applies to specs created on or after `adopted_at` in `.pose/policy/dor.json` (shipped empty), and `task_type` in the spec's frontmatter selects which sections it requires; once adopted, `check` applies it on the `→ in-progress` transition. Use `--all` to audit every spec; `--required-only` skips the optional `Decisions` section. **Lifecycle gate:** `status: done` requires `completed_at` and a valid disposition on every follow-up; for `spawned`/`covered`/`duplicate` the target must be an **existing** spec (and not itself). A target in another project is written qualified, `[covered: xref:<project>/spec:<slug>]`: when that project is bound (`POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS`) the spec must exist there, and when it is not bound the target is accepted, because the other repository being absent is not evidence the spec is missing. In any status it warns when an open follow-up's ownership is written outside the trailing `(owner:@alias crit:low|medium|high review:YYYY-MM-DD)` group, and it reads a wrapped follow-up bullet as one item, like `followups`.\n - `followups` — aggregates all specs' follow-ups, derives the live (`--open`) or full (`--all`) backlog, projects ownership (`--owner <alias>`) and expired reviews (`--overdue`), and proposes near-duplicate candidates by deterministic lexical similarity (token Jaccard + `SequenceMatcher`, stdlib only; threshold via `--similarity 0..100`, default 60). Exit 0 by default, no network; `--fail-overdue` turns expired reviews into a blocking, risk-based policy gate. The candidates are mechanical hints — the **semantic judgement** and the **confirmation of reuse** live in the agent layer (skill `pose-spec-closeout`), never in this script. Source coverage (spec `pose-source-coverage-integrity`): the read reports what it could not see. `--json` carries `coverage_complete` and `coverage` (each loss names the artifact, the kind, and the spec it belongs to when the file name or a readable header shows it), the human output prints `coverage=INCOMPLETE` and each unread file, and `--fail-overdue` fails instead of certifying \"none overdue\" over an incomplete read. The backlog, `--candidates`, the project state and `pose state --attention` count one unresolved set: an explicit `[open]` and a bullet with no disposition (Attention projects the latter as `followup-undisposed`). The same applies to `pose assess tech-debt`, whose report states the files scanned, the ones it could not read (incomplete) and the ones it leaves out on purpose (over the size limit, binary, generated), and finds a marker after a very long line; and to the review history: `pose review-check` and `pose review verify` do not take the latest readable attempt or attestation as the current one while an unreadable record that may be later exists, and `pose review record` refuses to choose the attempt it supersedes from a history with a hole. The obligation report marks a producer `incomplete` when it lost an element (an obligation refused by validation, a spec or review record it could not read).\n - `review-plan` / `review-check` / `closeout-check` — resolve a deterministic component-aware plan from governed metadata, typed overlays and a closed native-tool catalog, then validate immutable attempts against both scope and plan digests. Opt in explicitly with review-policy schema v2, `component_aware: true` and `component_aware_adopted_at`; preview the migration read-only with `pose review-plan <scope> --explain` before committing the policy. Completed attempts older than that component adoption date remain auditable, while open scopes require the new plan. `pose review record` is dry-run by default, accepts `--plan-digest` to reject drift and appends with `--apply`; recommended tools are never executed implicitly. Hierarchical closeout rolls approvals through `spec:`, `milestone:` and `roadmap:` scopes. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_plan`, `pose_closeout_state`. The JSON of `closeout-check` and `pose_closeout_state` types its blockers in `diagnostics` (code, domain, refs, satisfaction condition; text blockers the engine cannot type yet are carried as `opaque`) and its next action in `next_step`; readiness `waiting_on` entries carry a `code`, and auto-attest pendencies a `code`, `source` and `condition` (spec `pose-typed-producer-diagnostics`). The prose fields stay for people; consumers read the codes.\n-- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` needs a severity and an action, and `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n+- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by|limit]` needs a severity and an action, `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date, and `within-limit` needs the id of a sealed accepted limit as `limit` and a rationale. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n   **Raw history:** every history reader of structural review and of the delivery graph ignores replace refs and grafts; a repository with either gets other review inputs than before, so rerun `pose index` and reseal. A trailer change set that starts at the repository's first commit resolves against the empty tree.\n   **Removal witnesses:** a review subject path a later spec removed reads as that removal only while Git still shows it: the deleting commit on `HEAD`, read raw, with no later commit touching the path, in a clone that is not shallow. Otherwise preparation refuses and asks for `pose index`. A generation binds the change sets of `.pose/reports/history` as `recorded-change-sets`, so one published earlier reads as unbound until the next `pose index`.\n   **Children:** a milestone of one local member has no review of its own and stands on that spec's closed approval, and a roadmap may declare `unreviewed_milestones` for milestones whose members are all terminal, sealed by its own review. Otherwise a milestone or roadmap's approval stands only while the review of each child does: a child rejected, stale, with lost attestation history, or not closed under a done parent stops the parent verifying approved, in `review verify`, `review-check` and closeout, and the parent recovers with its child, with no reseal. Selection follows replacement lineage: a replacement of the same content comes before what it replaces whatever the seal times say, a `replaces` naming other content counts for nothing, and a replaced bundle's approval is not retained.\ndiff --git a/docs-site/docs/cli.md b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\nindex 212c45f8..287e9081 100644\n--- a/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n+++ b/docs-site/docs/cli.md\n@@ -136,13 +136,13 @@ as current, but named so a reviewer can tell carried-forward evidence apart.\n - `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records a disposition for a\n   required criterion: `passed`, `not-applicable` (a rationale is required) or\n   `finding` (the evidence slot names a finding this attestation records).\n-- `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]`\n+- `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by|limit]`\n   records a finding. Every finding needs a severity and an action. `resolved`\n   and `wont-fix` close it; `accepted-risk` closes it only for a severity the\n   sealed gates accept and only with an owner, a rationale and a review date;\n   `within-limit` closes it at any severity, only with the id of a limit the\n-  spec declares under `### Accepted limits` and the bundle sealed (its\n-  `limit` field) and a rationale; `open` and `changes-requested` block\n+  spec declares under `### Accepted limits` and the bundle sealed (the ninth\n+  field, `limit`) and a rationale; `open` and `changes-requested` block\n   closeout. A reviewer records it from a follow-up whose `limit` names that\n   sealed limit.\n - The decision must be `approved`, or `approved-with-reservations` where the\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\nindex 973059fc..81db72b0 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\n@@ -101,6 +101,7 @@ func cmdReviewCheck(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {\n \tif eval.Assurance != nil {\n \t\trender(stdout, stderr).Field(\"review.assurance\", posemodel.RenderReviewAssurance(*eval.Assurance))\n \t}\n+\trenderWithinLimitFindings(render(stdout, stderr), \"review\", eval.Current)\n \tfor _, warning := range eval.Warnings {\n \t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"[WARN] %s\\n\", warning)\n \t}\n@@ -324,6 +325,7 @@ func cmdCloseoutCheck(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int\n \tif state.Review.Assurance != nil {\n \t\trender(stdout, stderr).Field(\"closeout.review_assurance\", posemodel.RenderReviewAssurance(*state.Review.Assurance))\n \t}\n+\trenderWithinLimitFindings(render(stdout, stderr), \"closeout\", state.Review.Current)\n \tfor _, blocker := range state.Blockers {\n \t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, \"[ERROR] %s\\n\", blocker)\n \t}\n@@ -1274,7 +1276,7 @@ func parseCLIReviewFindings(values []string) ([]posemodel.ReviewFinding, error)\n \tfor _, raw := range values {\n \t\tparts := strings.Split(raw, \"|\")\n \t\tif len(parts) < 5 || posemodel.ValidateSlug(parts[0]) != nil {\n-\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"finding must be ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]\")\n+\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"finding must be ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by|limit]\")\n \t\t}\n \t\tfinding := posemodel.ReviewFinding{ID: parts[0], Severity: parts[1], Disposition: parts[2], Action: parts[3], Evidence: parts[4]}\n \t\tif len(parts) > 5 {\n@@ -1286,6 +1288,11 @@ func parseCLIReviewFindings(values []string) ([]posemodel.ReviewFinding, error)\n \t\tif len(parts) > 7 {\n \t\t\tfinding.ReviewBy = parts[7]\n \t\t}\n+\t\t// The accepted limit a within-limit finding falls inside (spec\n+\t\t// pose-review-declared-limits-followups R3).\n+\t\tif len(parts) > 8 {\n+\t\t\tfinding.Limit = parts[8]\n+\t\t}\n \t\tresult = append(result, finding)\n \t}\n \treturn result, nil\n@@ -2596,3 +2603,17 @@ func closeGateBlocker(root string, scope posemodel.ScopeRef) string {\n \t}\n \treturn \"\"\n }\n+\n+// renderWithinLimitFindings shows, in the text output, each finding the\n+// current review recorded within a declared accepted limit, as the JSON\n+// does (spec pose-review-declared-limits-followups R6).\n+func renderWithinLimitFindings(out *cliout.Renderer, prefix string, current *posemodel.ReviewAttempt) {\n+\tif current == nil {\n+\t\treturn\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, f := range current.Findings {\n+\t\tif f.Disposition == \"within-limit\" {\n+\t\t\tout.Field(prefix+\".within_limit\", fmt.Sprintf(\"%s limit:%s severity:%s rationale:%s\", f.ID, f.Limit, f.Severity, strings.ReplaceAll(f.Rationale, \"\\n\", \" \")))\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\nindex 713f2261..5702a69a 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n@@ -79,11 +79,19 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \t\t\tentries[len(entries)-1].bad = true\n \t\t}\n \t}\n-\ttext := body\n-\tif start := strings.Index(text, \"<!--\"); start >= 0 && !strings.Contains(text[start:], \"-->\") {\n+\t// Any unterminated comment, not only the first, hides what follows it\n+\t// (spec pose-review-declared-limits-followups R2).\n+\tif unterminatedHTMLComment(body) {\n \t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: an unterminated HTML comment hides the rest of the spec\")\n \t}\n-\ttext = stripHTMLComments(text)\n+\t// A comment inside the section, also one written in inline code, would\n+\t// change a limit's sealed text: the section is refused whole instead of\n+\t// guessing which is meant (R2, D1).\n+\tif commentInAcceptedLimitsSection(body) {\n+\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limits: the section holds an HTML comment marker; write the limits without comments\")\n+\t\treturn nil, problems\n+\t}\n+\ttext := stripLimitComments(body)\n \tfence := \"\"\n \tfor _, line := range strings.Split(text, \"\\n\") {\n \t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n@@ -103,6 +111,12 @@ func ParseAcceptedLimits(body string) ([]ReviewAcceptedLimit, []string) {\n \t\tif fence != \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif in && indent > 0 && len(entries) > 0 && indent > entries[len(entries)-1].indent && indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\t// A heading-like line indented under a limit: read as a heading it\n+\t\t\t// would end the section and cut the limit (R1).\n+\t\t\treport(line, \"is a heading inside a limit; write the limit as one item\")\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n \t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(trimmed, \"# \"))\n \t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n@@ -215,3 +229,94 @@ func sealedAcceptedLimit(bundle ReviewBundle, id string) bool {\n \t}\n \treturn false\n }\n+\n+// htmlCommentStart returns the index in s, from from on, of the next \"<!--\"\n+// that opens an HTML comment, or -1. One written in inline code (an odd\n+// number of backticks before it on its line) opens nothing: Markdown shows\n+// it as text, as a spec that names the marker does (review of\n+// pose-review-declared-limits-followups: its own R2 named it).\n+func htmlCommentStart(s string, from int) int {\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tk := strings.Index(s[from:], \"<!--\")\n+\t\tif k < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn -1\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tk += from\n+\t\tlineStart := strings.LastIndex(s[:k], \"\\n\") + 1\n+\t\tif strings.Count(s[lineStart:k], \"`\")%2 == 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn k\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfrom = k + 4\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// stripLimitComments removes the HTML comments of body, as Markdown hides\n+// them; an unterminated one hides the rest.\n+func stripLimitComments(body string) string {\n+\tvar b strings.Builder\n+\tfrom := 0\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tstart := htmlCommentStart(body, from)\n+\t\tif start < 0 {\n+\t\t\tb.WriteString(body[from:])\n+\t\t\treturn b.String()\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tb.WriteString(body[from:start])\n+\t\tend := strings.Index(body[start+4:], \"-->\")\n+\t\tif end < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn b.String()\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfrom = start + 4 + end + 3\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// unterminatedHTMLComment reports whether any comment body opens has no\n+// \"-->\" after it.\n+func unterminatedHTMLComment(body string) bool {\n+\tfrom := 0\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tstart := htmlCommentStart(body, from)\n+\t\tif start < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn false\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tend := strings.Index(body[start+4:], \"-->\")\n+\t\tif end < 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn true\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfrom = start + 4 + end + 3\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// commentInAcceptedLimitsSection reports whether a line of the accepted\n+// limits section, read before comments are stripped, holds a comment marker.\n+// A comment outside the section, and a section heading inside one, are not\n+// the section.\n+func commentInAcceptedLimitsSection(body string) bool {\n+\tin, inComment := false, false\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(body, \"\\n\") {\n+\t\tif inComment {\n+\t\t\tk := strings.Index(line, \"-->\")\n+\t\t\tif k < 0 {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tinComment = false\n+\t\t\tline = line[k+3:]\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif in && (strings.Contains(line, \"<!--\") || strings.Contains(line, \"-->\")) {\n+\t\t\treturn true\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif k := htmlCommentStart(line, 0); k >= 0 {\n+\t\t\tif !strings.Contains(line[k:], \"-->\") {\n+\t\t\t\tinComment = true\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n+\t\tindent := len(line) - len(strings.TrimLeft(line, \" \\t\"))\n+\t\tif indent <= 3 && markdownHeadingRE.MatchString(trimmed) {\n+\t\t\theading := strings.TrimSpace(strings.Trim(trimmed, \"# \"))\n+\t\t\tin = strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Accepted limits\") || strings.EqualFold(heading, \"Limites aceitos\")\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn false\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\nindex 056373dc..f835ba24 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_conclusions.go\n@@ -181,7 +181,11 @@ func reviewAttestationFromDraft(run ReviewRun, bundle ReviewBundle, draft Review\n \t\t// A follow-up the reviewer classified inside a declared accepted\n \t\t// limit is recorded as such, naming the limit (spec\n \t\t// pose-review-declared-threat-model R3).\n-\t\tif f.Disposition == \"follow-up\" && f.Limit != \"\" {\n+\t\t// Only a limit the bundle sealed: a draft of any decision naming\n+\t\t// another is recorded as the follow-up it otherwise is, so no\n+\t\t// attestation names a limit that does not exist (spec\n+\t\t// pose-review-declared-limits-followups R4).\n+\t\tif f.Disposition == \"follow-up\" && f.Limit != \"\" && sealedAcceptedLimit(bundle, f.Limit) {\n \t\t\tfinding.Disposition, finding.Limit, finding.Rationale, finding.Owner, finding.ReviewBy = \"within-limit\", f.Limit, f.Summary, f.Owner, f.ReviewBy\n \t\t}\n \t\tatt.Findings = append(att.Findings, finding)\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json\nindex f5d485d4..d30f8088 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json\n+++ b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-bundle.schema.json\n@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@\n         \"plan\": {\n           \"type\": \"object\",\n           \"required\": [\"plan_digest\", \"independence\", \"components\", \"criteria\", \"tools\"],\n-          \"properties\": {\"plan_digest\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/digest\"}}\n+          \"properties\": {\"plan_digest\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/digest\"}, \"limitations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"accepted_limits\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"$ref\": \"review-plan.schema.json#/$defs/acceptedLimit\"}}}\n         },\n         \"gates\": {\n           \"type\": \"object\",\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\nindex dc28d2d5..32653ca1 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\n+++ b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\n@@ -25,9 +25,17 @@\n     \"structure\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/structure\"},\n     \"warnings\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n     \"blockers\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n-    \"explain\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}\n+    \"explain\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n+    \"limitations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n+    \"accepted_limits\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/acceptedLimit\"}}\n   },\n   \"$defs\": {\n+    \"acceptedLimit\": {\n+      \"type\": \"object\",\n+      \"additionalProperties\": true,\n+      \"required\": [\"id\", \"text\"],\n+      \"properties\": {\"id\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^([a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*#)?L[1-9][0-9]*$\"}, \"text\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"spec\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}\n+    },\n     \"component\": {\n       \"type\": \"object\",\n       \"additionalProperties\": true,","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01D6sqXxW4yezGmcjpyDjvDz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
